      "id": 1,
      "text": "Grand Cane was founded in 1881 when Amanda Hobgood deeded land to the New Orleans & Pacific Railway, laying out the village's streets.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand_Cane%2C_Louisiana"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Grand Cane is home to Louisiana's first accredited public high school, a landmark that helped shape the town's early prosperity.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand_Cane%2C_Louisiana"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In the 1990s, the village used a rural development grant to buy the old Hicks & Richardson general store, kicking off a 20‑year building‑buying campaign.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12,
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://discoverlouisianatravel.com/grand-cane-louisiana-small-town-comeback/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Today, Grand Cane leases nearly every historic Main Street building to local merchants for just 25 cents per square foot, even covering their electric bills.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://discoverlouisianatravel.com/grand-cane-louisiana-small-town-comeback/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The historic Cook‑Hill House, now a bed‑and‑breakfast, is one of the town’s preserved landmarks that draws visitors to its century‑old charm.",
